Subject: [PATCH] sigdelayed: reserve the red zone before building the frame

The psABI reserves 128 bytes below %rsp and a signal delivery is one of the
two things that may not touch them; a kernel skips them before it builds a
handler frame. Cygwin's sigdelayed does not. Its first instruction is
`pushq %r10`, which writes at %rsp-8, and spike 3 measured that word lost on
every single delivery.

Two lines. `subq $128,%rsp` before the first push moves the whole frame below
the reserved bytes, and `ret $128` releases them on the way out.

`ret $128` rather than an `addq` because at that point in the epilogue every
register has been restored and there is no scratch left to compute with. `ret
imm16` pops the return address and then adds the immediate to %rsp, which is
exactly the operation wanted and is why the instruction exists. The 128 bytes
cannot be borrowed as scratch either: they are the red zone, and using them
would defeat the patch.

`.seh_stackalloc 128` keeps the unwind data honest. Without it an unwinder
walking out of a handler computes a frame 128 bytes off, and Cygwin's own
exception machinery rides SEH.

Everything else in the routine is untouched, including the `.seh_setframe
%rbp,0` further down: the frame is shifted down uniformly, and that directive
records %rbp against %rsp at a point where both have moved together.

Against newlib-cygwin at cygwin-3.6.10. sigdelayed lives in a generator
rather than in a .S file, so the patch is against scripts/gendef.

--- a/winsup/cygwin/scripts/gendef
+++ b/winsup/cygwin/scripts/gendef
@@ -184,6 +184,11 @@
 	.global	sigdelayed
 	.seh_proc sigdelayed
 sigdelayed:
+	# Skip the psABI red zone before touching the interrupted stack. The
+	# push below would otherwise land at %rsp-8, which is inside it.
+	subq	\$128,%rsp
+	.seh_stackalloc 128
+
 	pushq	%r10				# used for return address injection
 	.seh_pushreg %r10
 	pushq	%rbp
@@ -252,7 +257,9 @@
 	popf
 	popq	%rbp
 	xchgq	%r10,(%rsp)
-	ret
+	# Pop the return address and release the reservation in one step; no
+	# register is free here to add with.
+	ret	\$128
 	.seh_endproc
 _sigdelayed_end:
 	.global _sigdelayed_end
